WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:15.170
<v Ira Glass>OK America, prepare to have your life change for the better. There is a situation that you find yourself in all the time. And it has been unclear what to say or do in that situation until now.
<v Ira Glass>好了，美国，准备好迎接你们的生活向更好的方向转变吧。有一种情况你们总是会遇到。直到现在，在这种情况下该说什么或做什么还不清楚。

00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:26.420
<v Nancy Updike>We heard about it, and it was like Post-its. It was just all of the sudden something that you needed to use all the time. It just explained so much.
<v Nancy Updike>我们听说了这件事，它就像便利贴一样。突然之间，它就成了你需要一直使用的东西。它解释了太多事情。

00:00:26.420 --> 00:01:06.810
<v Ira Glass>That's Nancy Updike, one of the regular contributors to our radio show. And she tells the story this way. A couple years ago, friends of hers were traveling in Europe, and they are walking through these old buildings. And these people do not know anything special about architecture. But you know how it is when you're a tourist. They're walking through these buildings, and they're looking at the doorways and the tiles. And they decide that they think that this one building has a very Moorish influence. And they're pointing out details, and saying the Moors this, and the Moors that. And finally, one of them turns to the other and says, you know what? We sound like we're in a magazine, a magazine called Modern Jackass.
<v Ira Glass>那是南希·厄普代克，我们广播节目的常驻撰稿人之一。她是这样讲这个故事的：几年前，她的朋友们在欧洲旅行，走在这些古老的建筑中。这些人对建筑一无所知。但你们知道作为游客是什么感觉。他们走在这些建筑里，看着门框和瓷砖。他们认定这座建筑有非常浓厚的摩尔风格。他们指着细节，说着“摩尔人这个”、“摩尔人那个”。最后，其中一个人转向另一个人说：“你知道吗？我们听起来就像是在一本杂志里，一本叫《现代混蛋》的杂志。”

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:23.080
<v Ira Glass>Modern Jackass. Of course, there is no Modern Jackass . But ever since I heard that story, I found myself referring to Modern Jackass all the time. It's incredibly useful, and it could be useful to you, to back out of all kinds of awkward conversational situations.
<v Ira Glass>《现代混蛋》。当然，并没有《现代混蛋》这本杂志。但自从我听到那个故事后，我发现自己一直在引用《现代混蛋》。它非常有用，对你来说也可能很有用，可以帮你摆脱各种尴尬的谈话局面。

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:34.710
<v Nancy Updike>The thing about Modern Jackass is, it's usually not something about which you know nothing. It's something about which you know a little bit, enough to sort of get yourself into trouble.
<v Nancy Updike>关于《现代混蛋》的一点是，这通常不是你一无所知的事情。而是你稍微了解一点点的事情，足以让你陷入麻烦。

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:36.400
<v Ira Glass>Like you read an article.
<v Ira Glass>就像你读过一篇文章。

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:39.570
<v Nancy Updike>Exactly, or something on the web.
<v Nancy Updike>没错，或者在网上看到过什么。

00:01:39.570 --> 00:02:23.560
<v Ira Glass>Just last weekend, I was out for breakfast with some friends. And we got into this conversation about these people who do caloric restriction. Have you heard about this? Apparently, there are these people who believe that if you eat a lot less, it can make you live longer. As so we're talking about this, and somebody's explaining the cells of your body go through all this wear and tear when you actually digest food. And before you know it, one of my friends-- somebody who knows nothing about biology, actually-- starts talking about mitochondria. Mitochondria. And maybe he had a little bit of knowledge about this. But it was totally Modern Jackass . Modern Jackass , the medical edition, which Nancy says that she finds herself in quite a bit.
<v Ira Glass>就在上周末，我和一些朋友出去吃早餐。我们聊起了那些进行热量限制的人。你听说过这个吗？显然，有些人相信如果你吃得少得多，就能活得更久。当我们谈论这个时，有人解释说，当你消化食物时，你的身体细胞会经历各种磨损。没过多久，我的一个朋友——其实他对生物学一无所知——就开始谈论线粒体。线粒体。也许他对此有一点点了解。但这完全是《现代混蛋》，医学版。南希说她发现自己经常陷入这种情况。

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:49.470
<v Nancy Updike>My mother sends me information about partially hydrogenated oils. And then when somebody says, wait, why is partially hydrogenated oil bad again? I say, well, it's an unstable compound, which it is. It's oil to which hydrogen has been added in order to make it solid at room temperature. That I know. That's a fact.
<v Nancy Updike>我母亲给我发送关于部分氢化油的信息。然后当有人说：“等等，为什么部分氢化油又是不好的？”我说：“嗯，它是一种不稳定的化合物，事实确实如此。”它是为了让油在室温下呈固态而添加了氢的油。这一点我知道。这是一个事实。

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:51.350
<v Ira Glass>And why would that be bad, Nancy?
<v Ira Glass>南希，为什么那会不好呢？

00:02:51.350 --> 00:03:02.900
<v Nancy Updike>Well, that's where we get into Modern Jackass territory. It's unstable in your body. There's an extra hydrogen atom that can interact with things.
<v Nancy Updike>嗯，这就是我们进入《现代混蛋》领域的地方。它在你的体内不稳定。有一个额外的氢原子可以与事物发生相互作用。

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:05.910
<v Ira Glass>Oxygen and form water.
<v Ira Glass>与氧气反应形成水。

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:13.140
<v Nancy Updike>Exactly.
<v Nancy Updike>完全正确。

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:42.080
<v Ira Glass>Having no information, that's one thing. That's pure. The trouble is when you have a little bit of information about the problems with electronic voting machines, what it really means, the battle between carbs and protein. It's hard for some people not to take the tiny pigment contained in that tiny bit of information and paint a vast canvas of the entire world. It is exactly the problem that people encounter in every story in our program today.
<v Ira Glass>一无所知是一回事。那是纯粹的。麻烦在于当你对电子投票机的弊端、它真正的含义、碳水化合物和蛋白质之间的争论有一点了解时。有些人很难不拿那一点点知识里包含的微小碎片，去描绘整个世界的宏大图景。这正是我们今天节目中每个人在每个故事中所遇到的问题。

00:03:42.080 --> 00:04:16.100
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life, from WBEZ Chicago, distributed by Public Radio International. Today we bring you four stories of people taking a smidgen, a tiny smidgen of understanding, and stretching it far past the breaking point. Because as everybody knows, all over the world, a little bit of knowledge can be a very entertaining thing. We have Dan Savage matching wits with a six year old boy. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>这是来自芝加哥WBEZ的《美国生活》，由国际公共广播电台发行。今天我们带给你们四个故事，讲述人们拿着一点点，一点点微小的理解，并将其延伸到远远超出临界点的程度。因为正如每个人所知，在世界各地，一点点知识可能是一件非常有趣的事情。我们有丹·萨维奇与一个六岁男孩斗智斗勇。请继续收听。

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:30.390
<v Ira Glass>Act One, When Small Thoughts Meet Big Brains. We have this story about people functioning with a tiny little bit of knowledge, long past the point that you think they would, from Alex Blumberg.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，当小思想遇到大脑袋。我们有这个故事，讲述人们带着一点点微小的知识，在远远超出你认为他们会停止的程度之后，依然继续运作，来自亚历克斯·布隆伯格。

00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:56.050
<v Alex Blumberg>I can reconstruct the events that led me to one of the most embarrassing conversations of my adult life. The chain starts back when I was 11 or 12, and I first heard the term Nielsen family. I was probably listening to some adults talk. And from their conversation I gathered that networks consulted Nielsen families to find out how popular a television show was. But that didn't make sense. Why would they only ask people named Nielsen which shows they liked. I started thinking.
<v Alex Blumberg>我可以重现导致我成年生活中最尴尬谈话之一的事件。这条链条始于我11或12岁时，我第一次听到“尼尔森家庭”这个词。我可能是在听一些成年人交谈。从他们的谈话中，我推断出电视网咨询尼尔森家庭来了解电视节目的受欢迎程度。但这说不通。为什么他们只问姓尼尔森的人喜欢什么节目？我开始思考。

00:04:56.050 --> 00:05:30.840
<v Alex Blumberg>I knew that when they figured things like this out, they didn't ask everybody, they just asked a small percentage of people, and then extrapolated. I think I figured they had done some research and found that the name Nielsen-- because it was a common name maybe, and it seemed to cut across class and economic lines-- actually came pretty close to a representative sample. I knew this wasn't the way they measured public opinion now, but it seemed like the Nielsen surveys had been around for a while. And I figured they were just a holdover from a more primitive, less statistically rigorous time. After that, I really didn't think about it again. Or if I did, it was only with a mild curiosity. I wonder why TV still does it that way?
<v Alex Blumberg>我知道当他们计算这类事情时，他们不会问每个人，他们只是问一小部分人，然后进行推断。我想我推断他们做了一些研究，发现“尼尔森”这个名字——也许因为它是一个常见的名字，而且看起来跨越了阶级和经济界限——实际上相当接近一个代表性样本。我知道这并不是他们现在衡量公众舆论的方式，但看起来尼尔森调查已经存在一段时间了。我以为它们只是一个更原始、统计上不那么严谨时代的遗留物。在那之后，我真的没有再想过它。或者如果我想过，也只是带着一种轻微的好奇。我想知道为什么电视仍然那样做？

00:05:30.840 --> 00:06:06.090
<v Alex Blumberg>Fast forward 20 years. I was talking with a friend of mine, who was telling me about her friend, who had been selected to be a Nielsen family. And I said to her, isn't that weird that they're all named Nielsen? My friend looked at me for what seemed like a long time. Somewhere during her very long pause-- because of the very long pause, in fact-- I realized, of course they're not all named Nielsen. That makes no sense at all. At the time of this conversation, I was 34 years old, and I couldn't believe I had gotten this far without ever stopping to think it through. It made me wonder what else I'd missed, and if this has ever happened to anyone besides me.
<v Alex Blumberg>快进20年。我和我的一个朋友交谈，她告诉我她的朋友被选为尼尔森家庭。我对她说：“他们都姓尼尔森，这不是很奇怪吗？”我的朋友看了我一眼，那眼神似乎持续了很长时间。在她那非常长的停顿期间——事实上，正因为那非常长的停顿——我意识到，当然他们并非都姓尼尔森。那根本说不通。在那次谈话时，我34岁了，我无法相信我竟然活到了这个岁数却从未停下来思考清楚。这让我怀疑我还错过了什么，以及这种情况是否曾经发生在除我之外的任何人身上。

00:06:06.090 --> 00:06:24.560
<v Jodie Mace>When I was a kid, and I would see the school crossing signs, and there's the picture of the little kids walking, and it would say school x-ing And I thought that the x-ing was a word. And I pronounced it zing.
<v Jodie Mace>小时候，当我看到学校的人行横道标志时，上面有小孩子走路的图片，写着“学校 X-ing”，我以为“x-ing”是一个单词。我把它读成“zing”（嗖）。

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:40.430
<v Alex Blumberg>Turns out, I'm not alone. I've been talking to people about this for weeks. And there are a lot of us out there-- like me and this woman, Jodie Mace-- carting around our childhood beliefs well into adulthood. Jodie thought there were lots of zings, deer zings, railroad zings. That makes sense.
<v Alex Blumberg>事实证明，我并不孤单。几周来我一直在和人们谈论这件事。我们中间有很多人——就像我和这位女士，朱迪·梅斯——把我们的童年信念一直带到了成年。朱迪以为有很多“zing”，鹿“zing”，铁路“zing”。这倒说得通。

00:06:40.430 --> 00:07:24.840
<v Jodie Mace>When I was in my 20s, and I was walking into work, and about 10 geese walked in front of me on the sidewalk. So I just turned to my coworker and casually said, it looks like they should have a zing sign there for the geese. There was a sort of long, awkward silence. And I thought that he was thinking, you know, that really is a good idea. But instead, he finally said, you know, zing isn't a word.
<v Jodie Mace>我20多岁时，正走去上班，大约10只鹅走在人行道上我面前。所以我只是转向我的同事，随口说：“看起来它们应该在那里有个‘zing’标志给鹅。”有一种很长的、尴尬的沉默。我以为他在想，你知道，这确实是个好主意。但相反，他终于说：“你知道，‘zing’不是一个单词。”

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:35.570
<v Alex Blumberg>In talking to people, I found out that a lot of these lingering misconceptions involve mispronunciation. And often, the mispronunciation survives into adulthood because the mistake just sounds better, or makes more sense.
<v Alex Blumberg>在与人交谈中，我发现许多这些挥之不去的误解涉及发音错误。而且通常，这种发音错误会延续到成年，因为错误的发音听起来更好，或者更有道理。

00:07:35.570 --> 00:07:49.340
<v Jodie Mace>It should be a word, and it should be zing. You don't want a kid to walk slowly across the crossing. If he's smart, he's going to zing.
<v Jodie Mace>它应该是一个词，而且它应该是“zing”。你不想让孩子在过马路时走得慢。如果他聪明，他就会“zing”地跑过去。

00:07:49.340 --> 00:08:16.140
<v Alex Blumberg>Consider the word misled. I talked to three people, including my own father, who used to pronounce it mizeled. All three believed it was the past tense of a nonexistent verb, mizel, which means to deceive or to mislead. There's another guy I spoke to who thought, well into his early 20s, that the word quesadilla was Spanish for what's the deal?
<v Alex Blumberg>考虑“misled”（被误导）这个词。我和三个人谈过，包括我自己的父亲，他们过去常把它读成“mizeled”。所有三个人都认为它是动词“mizel”的过去式，这个动词不存在，意思是指欺骗或误导。我还和另一个人谈过，他到20岁出头还认为“quesadilla”（墨西哥油炸玉米饼）在西班牙语里是“这是什么情况？”的意思。

00:08:16.140 --> 00:08:29.760
<v Alex Blumberg>Most of the common childhood myths, like that babies come from storks, get corrected sooner or later. They're not obscure enough to sneak into adulthood unscrutinized. But occasionally, even a very popular childhood myth can make it through, like unicorns.
<v Alex Blumberg>大多数常见的童年神话，比如婴儿来自鹳鸟，迟早会被纠正。它们不够晦涩，不足以在不被审查的情况下溜进成年期。但偶尔，即使是一个非常受欢迎的童年神话也能幸存下来，比如独角兽。

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:33.710
<v Kristy Kruger>In my head, a unicorn wasn't really any different than a zebra.
<v Kristy Kruger>在我脑海里，独角兽和斑马没什么不同。

00:08:33.710 --> 00:08:35.110
<v Alex Blumberg>This is Kristy Kruger.
<v Alex Blumberg>这是克里斯蒂·克鲁格。

00:08:35.110 --> 00:08:42.220
<v Kristy Kruger>I mean, in terms of believability, I think the unicorn is really ahead of the dinosaur.
<v Kristy Kruger>我是说，就可信度而言，我认为独角兽比恐龙真的要领先。

00:08:42.220 --> 00:08:43.950
<v Alex Blumberg>What do you mean?
<v Alex Blumberg>你是什么意思？

00:08:43.950 --> 00:09:03.630
<v Kristy Kruger>Well, I mean, when you think about a dinosaur from a kid's perspective, a dinosaur is these really large, monstrous animals roaming the Earth. And then you have a unicorn, which is basically just a horse with a horn.
<v Kristy Kruger>嗯，我是说，当你从孩子的角度思考恐龙时，恐龙是那些在地球上漫游的真正巨大、可怕的动物。然后你有独角兽，它基本上就是一匹长角的马。

00:09:03.630 --> 00:09:16.010
<v Alex Blumberg>As Kristy Kruger grew up, she says that if she ever thought about unicorns, they were on a grassy plane somewhere in Africa, drinking from a watering hole with the wildebeest and the impala. And then one night, she found herself in a conversation at a party.
<v Alex Blumberg>随着克里斯蒂·克鲁格长大，她说如果她曾经想到独角兽，它们是在非洲某处的草地上，与角马和黑斑羚一起在水坑边喝水。然后一天晚上，她发现自己在派对上进行了一场谈话。

00:09:16.010 --> 00:09:42.820
<v Kristy Kruger>It was about a group of five to seven people, kind of standing around the keg, just talking. And somehow a discussion of endangered species came up, in which I posed the question, is the unicorn endangered or extinct? And basically, there was a big gap of silence.
<v Kristy Kruger>那大约是五到七个人围在桶边聊天。不知怎么地，谈到了濒危物种，我提出了一个问题：“独角兽是濒危还是灭绝了？”基本上，出现了一个巨大的沉默空档。

00:09:42.820 --> 00:09:47.390
<v Alex Blumberg>As you might be gathering, at some point in all these stories, you come to a big gap of silence.
<v Alex Blumberg>正如你们可能正在收集的信息，在所有这些故事的某个时刻，你都会遇到一个巨大的沉默空档。

00:09:47.390 --> 00:10:12.720
<v Kristy Kruger>And then everybody laughed. And then that laughter was followed by more silence when they realized I wasn't laughing. And I was like, yeah, oh God, unicorns aren't real? Oh no.
<v Kristy Kruger>然后每个人都笑了。然后那种笑声之后是更多的沉默，因为他们意识到我并没有笑。我说：“是啊，噢天哪，独角兽不是真的？噢，不。”

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:31.620
<v Alex Blumberg>Sometimes a ridiculous belief will survive into adulthood, and it's our parents who are to blame. Robin didn't think there was anything strange about the way she was raised. She lived together with her sister and her parents in a nice house in the suburbs. She went to school like the other kids, watched TV and did her homework. And she ate the exact same thing for dinner every night of her life, baked chicken.
<v Alex Blumberg>有时一个荒谬的信念会存活到成年，而这要怪我们的父母。罗宾不认为她的成长方式有什么奇怪的。她和她的姐妹及父母住在郊区一栋不错的房子里。她像其他孩子一样上学，看电视，做家庭作业。而且她每天晚餐都吃同样的东西，烤鸡。

00:10:31.620 --> 00:11:12.610
<v Robin>It was like Monday, chicken. Tuesday, chicken. Wednesday, chicken. Thursday, Friday, Saturday, Sunday, chicken, chicken chicken, chicken, chicken, every night of my life until I left for college. At the end of the first week of college-- when everyone's desperately trying to fit in, and it's important that you act cool and sophisticated and whatever-- everyone begins complaining about the food that we're being served. What was the hard stuff in the sloppy joe? What was that mystery meat? What animal did it come from?
<v Robin>就像周一，鸡肉。周二，鸡肉。周三，鸡肉。周四、周五、周六、周日，鸡肉、鸡肉、鸡肉、鸡肉、鸡肉，在我离开去上大学之前的每一天晚上。大学第一周结束时——当每个人都在拼命想融入，而且表现得酷、世俗等等很重要的时候——每个人都开始抱怨学校提供的食物。那种糊状肉三明治（sloppy joe）里的硬东西是什么？那种神秘肉是什么？它来自什么动物？

00:11:12.610 --> 00:11:52.170
<v Robin>And I'm looking at these people like they are crazy. The variety we are getting here every night. Every night there's a different meal. One night it's mac and cheese. One night it's mystery meat. One night it's sloppy joe. I was like, how can you criticize? I mean, it's a testament to what great chefs they must be that they can make a different meal every single night of the week. And they just kind of stared. And they're like, what? And I'm like, what, what?
<v Robin>我看着这些人，就像他们疯了一样。我们每天晚上得到的种类。每天晚上都是不同的餐点。一晚是通心粉加奶酪。一晚是神秘肉。一晚是糊状肉三明治。我想，怎么能批评呢？我的意思是，这证明了他们一定是多么伟大的厨师，才能在每周的每个晚上都做出不同的餐点。他们只是盯着看。他们说：“什么？”我说：“什么，什么？”

00:11:52.170 --> 00:12:15.520
<v Robin>What's running through my head is, wait a minute, these people are implying that they had variation in their meal plan for their entire life. It's mind-bending. I mean, I don't care what I learned throughout college. This is the revelation that has stuck with me. This is what I've learned. All of a sudden like, holy God.
<v Robin>我脑子里想的是，等一下，这些人暗示他们一生中餐点都有变化。这太令人费解了。我的意思是，我不在乎我在大学里学到了什么。这是一直伴随着我的启示。这就是我学到的。突然之间就像，神啊。

00:12:15.520 --> 00:12:40.500
<v Alex Blumberg>When Robin came home for Thanksgiving that year, and confronted her mother with the startling fact that everyone else ate things besides chicken growing up, her mother just shrugged her shoulders and said, you liked chicken. Robin had to concede the point. Even when they'd gone out to restaurants, Robin ordered chicken. They all had.
<v Alex Blumberg>当罗宾那年感恩节回家，并向她母亲面对那个惊人的事实，即其他人成长过程中除了鸡肉还吃别的东西时，她母亲只是耸了耸肩说：“你喜欢吃鸡肉。”罗宾不得不承认这一点。即使他们去餐馆，罗宾点的也是鸡肉。他们全家都这样。

00:12:40.500 --> 00:13:00.310
<v Alex Blumberg>Here's one more. When Harriet Lerner was a girl, her family was going through some lean years. There were two kids, the house needed repairs. There wasn't much money for holiday gifts. Harriet was seven and she wanted a bike. Her sister Susan was 12. She wanted a set of encyclopedias. But when they came downstairs on Christmas morning, there were only two small boxes waiting for them.
<v Alex Blumberg>再说一个。当哈丽特·勒纳还是个女孩时，她的家庭经历了一些贫困的年份。家里有两个孩子，房子需要修理。节日礼物没多少钱。哈丽特七岁，想要一辆自行车。她的妹妹苏珊12岁。她想要一套百科全书。但当她们圣诞节早上走下楼时，只有两个小盒子等着她们。

00:13:00.310 --> 00:13:51.380
<v Harriet Lerner>What was inside them-- and we both had exactly the same gift-- were these real ugly, metal tissue holders painted black, with these corny red and yellow roses. They were painted with these cheesy looking red and yellow roses. And I looked at my tissue box, and I started to cry. And I looked at my big sister Susan, and I thought, of course she was going to cry too. And she looked like maybe she was going to cry. But then she sort of put on a big smile. And then she told me that the boxes were painted by trained monkeys.
<v Harriet Lerner>盒子里装的是什么——我们俩收到的礼物完全一样——是这些真正丑陋、漆成黑色的金属纸巾盒，上面有俗气的红黄玫瑰。它们被漆上了那些看起来俗气的红黄玫瑰。我看着我的纸巾盒，开始哭。我看着我的姐姐苏珊，我想，她当然也会哭。她看起来好像要哭了。但后来她露出了一个大大的微笑。然后她告诉我，这些盒子是由训练有素的猴子画的。

00:13:51.380 --> 00:14:10.530
<v Alex Blumberg>The box became Harriet's prized possession. She kept it on display in her room through elementary school, through high school. Her friends asked her about it, she'd say, oh yeah, it was painted by trained monkeys. Nobody ever challenged her on it, maybe because she believed it herself so completely. And then one day, she was home from college, back in the house where she grew up.
<v Alex Blumberg>这个盒子成了哈丽特最珍贵的财产。她在小学、高中期间一直把它放在房间里展示。朋友们问她关于它的事，她说：“哦，是的，这是由训练有素的猴子画的。”从来没有人挑战过她，也许是因为她自己完全相信这一点。然后有一天，她从大学回家，回到她长大的房子里。

00:14:10.530 --> 00:15:02.750
<v Harriet Lerner>And I'm going through some papers, or maybe I was snooping through Susan's papers, and I found a composition, and it had her name on it. And she had written it in high school. And it was called "The Tissue Box Story." So I sat down on the floor of Susan's bedroom to read this composition. And Susan told the story just as I told it, except that she wrote how she felt when she saw me crying. And how she then looked at my parents, and saw that my mother was about to cry too. And how she looked at the tissue boxes, and then she remembered that my father had a friend who made them. And she knew how much my parents hated taking charity.
<v Harriet Lerner>我正在翻看一些文件，或者也许我在翻看苏珊的文件，我发现了一篇作文，上面有她的名字。那是她在高中时写的。它叫“纸巾盒的故事”。所以我坐在苏珊卧室的地板上读这篇作文。苏珊讲的故事和我讲的一模一样，除了她写了当我看到我哭时她是什么感受。以及她后来怎么看着我的父母，看到我母亲也要哭了。以及她看着那些纸巾盒，然后她记得我父亲有个朋友做了这些。她知道我父母有多讨厌接受施舍。

00:15:02.750 --> 00:16:01.700
<v Harriet Lerner>And suddenly, even though she was about to cry, she forced herself to smile. And she pretended those boxes were painted by trained monkeys. And of course, I didn't know any of this. But the funny thing she wrote in her composition is that she just rushed upstairs and started crying all over her pillow. And she wasn't really sad about the gift really, is what she said in the composition. She wasn't sure why she was crying, except that it was sort of like she had volunteered to be a grown up before she was even ready for it.
<v Harriet Lerner>突然之间，尽管她当时也要哭，她还是强迫自己微笑。她假装那些盒子是训练有素的猴子画的。当然，我不知道这些。但她在作文里写的有趣的事情是，她只是冲上楼梯，在枕头上大哭起来。她说那不是因为礼物，并不是真的因为礼物，她是这么写的。她不确定自己为什么哭，只是觉得这就像她在还没准备好之前就志愿成为一个成年人。

00:16:01.700 --> 00:16:21.430
<v Harriet Lerner>Up until that moment, I had never thought to question my sister's story. I had never subjected it to the scrutiny of a grown up mind. I mean, I was 20. I don't know, I had this tissue box that was painted by trained monkeys. And then it wasn't painted by trained monkeys, really.
<v Harriet Lerner>直到那一刻，我从未想过质疑我姐姐的故事。我从未把它置于成年人心智的审视之下。我的意思是，我20岁了。我不知道，我有这个由训练有素的猴子画的纸巾盒。然后它实际上不是由训练有素的猴子画的。

00:16:21.430 --> 00:16:45.730
<v Alex Blumberg>Up until reading that story, Harriet thought that her sisters lies had been only to torment her, like the time Harriet swallowed an apple seed, and her big sister convinced her that she had an apple tree growing inside her. She had always been jealous of her sister, always wanted to be the big sister. But reading her sister's story that day made her realize how responsible her sister felt for her, and for their entire family, and how there were benefits to being the baby. It was good to learn all that.
<v Alex Blumberg>读到那个故事之前，哈丽特以为她姐姐的谎言只是为了折磨她，比如哈丽特吞下一个苹果籽的那次，她姐姐说服她，说她肚子里长了一棵苹果树。她一直嫉妒她的姐姐，一直想成为那个大姐姐。但那天读到她姐姐的故事让她意识到她姐姐对她，以及对他们整个家庭感到多么负责，以及作为最小的孩子有什么好处。学到这一切真好。

00:16:45.730 --> 00:16:51.210
<v Alex Blumberg>But the vision of the lie-- that we live in a world where monkeys can be trained to paint-- is hard to give up.
<v Alex Blumberg>但是那个谎言的愿景——我们生活在一个猴子可以被训练去绘画的世界里——很难放弃。

00:16:51.210 --> 00:17:03.280
<v Harriet Lerner>And really, it's just that I can still picture this tissue box, and how much I loved it, this tissue box painted by trained monkeys.
<v Harriet Lerner>而且真的，只是我仍然能想象这个纸巾盒，以及我是多么爱它，这个由训练有素的猴子画的纸巾盒。

00:17:03.280 --> 00:17:39.400
<v Alex Blumberg>I know what she means. For me, there's something appealingly weird about a world where only people who happened to have been born with the name Nielsen get to decide what goes on television. And not long after the day that Jodie Mace's coworker set her straight about the word zing, she found herself on the opposite side of the exact same situation. She was having a conversation with another coworker, and he asked her if elves were real. Elves? Like that live in the forest, she asked, with the pointy toes? He nodded. She paused. And then she said, yeah, of course they are.
<v Alex Blumberg>我知道她的意思。对我来说，在一个只有恰好出生时叫尼尔森这个名字的人才能决定电视上播什么的世界里，有些古怪之处，这很有吸引力。在朱迪·梅斯的同事告诉她关于“zing”这个词后的不久，她发现自己处于完全相同情况的对立面。她和另一个同事交谈，他问她精灵是否是真的。精灵？就像住在森林里那种，脚趾尖尖的那种？他点点头。她停顿了一下。然后她说：“是啊，当然是真的。”

